\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}Standard number systems are denoted by $\mathbb N,\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$; index ranges, computational models and specialized notation follow the cited problem source and the definitions below.


In the linear interpolation setting, data form a matrix $X$ and response vector $y$. For a specified norm on the parameter space, a minimum-norm interpolator chooses $\widehat\beta\in\arg\min\{\lVert\beta\rVert:X\beta=y\}$. Interpolation means zero training residual, possibly fitting noise. Prediction risk is expected loss on a fresh observation from the specified data law; excess risk subtracts the population benchmark. Benign overfitting means that excess risk tends to zero along the source asymptotic sequence even though the observations are interpolated. Geometry includes the norm ball and its relation to the design and noise distributions.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Identify necessary and sufficient geometric conditions under which, for an arbitrary norm, the associated minimum-norm interpolating estimator exhibits benign overfitting. \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2023/41}{[S2]}
\paragraph{Scope qualification.}
Sample-size/dimension scaling, noise law, loss and tie-breaking among minimizers are part of the estimator model. A universal criterion is requested, rather than a claim that arbitrary norms automatically yield consistent interpolation.
\paragraph{Source status.}
The supplied catalogue labels this record partially solved. Its source-status metadata was checked against the supplied snapshot on 2026-10-11. This import does not independently establish current open status or mathematical correctness. The catalogue literature assessment is dated 2026-08-22.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Which geometric properties exactly determine whether minimum-norm interpolation generalizes despite fitting noisy data?

